# Keep all notebook state in memory

## Scope and existing evidence

Hold documents, assets, undo history, previews, and derived plans only for the
active session with no database, autosave, hidden recovery file, or cloud copy.

Current executable evidence composes source-preparation draft text, accepted
semantic notebook revisions, and semantic Undo/Redo history under one
`SessionApplication` instance owned by the live process. Transport-neutral
command review, exact and caller-bounded batch analysis and simulation,
selection analysis, and atomic batch application also route through that owner
without exposing the concrete semantic service.

The localhost runtime still consumes only the established draft inbound port,
while the same application owner retains accepted semantic and history
authority for later admitted routes. `SessionApplication` is the only production
consumer of the concrete semantic-session service. No command transport or
writable scope is published by this composition evidence.

Dropping that owner and creating a fresh application yields empty draft fields,
no accepted revision, no history position, and no retained asset bytes; its
Debug projection does not expose private draft text or raw media. The active
application can retain already-validated raw bytes only after an accepted
semantic asset identity exists. It preserves the first retained byte sequence
for that identity and keeps those bytes process-local across semantic Undo/Redo.

After Undo followed by a new candidate branch, abandoned asset bytes cannot
reattach: the old accepted asset identity is absent from the new revision, Redo
is discarded, and newly accepted assets receive distinct identities with no
retained bytes. The process owner now also prunes retained bytes whose accepted
asset identities are unreachable from current, Undo, and Redo semantic state.
No-op batches, rejected candidates, bounded command rejection, and stale direct
edits preserve both Redo and its reachable bytes. Branch cleanup is selective:
bytes still reachable through current or Undo state remain retained.

All semantic mutation entry points in `SessionApplication` now share one private
branch-cleanup gate so this lifecycle rule is not duplicated per operation.

Formula, physical-page-profile, table-cell-span, and table-row-role replacements
are exercised through that same gate after Undo with Redo-only asset bytes:
successful changes prune the discarded branch, while exact-value no-ops preserve
Redo and its retained bytes. Malformed mathematics, invalid page geometry, and
invalid table grids also reject without dropping that branch or its bytes.
Direct text and bounded-batch branches are covered separately. Grapheme-range
editing now also preserves Redo and its reachable bytes on provider rejection
or semantic no-op, while an applied grapheme mutation prunes the abandoned
Redo-only bytes through the same gate.

Read-only grapheme position, signed-step, and selection inspection also route
through `SessionApplication`; semantic revision/target rejection occurs before
the boundary provider is consulted and cannot mutate history. Edit, Undo, and
Redo each invalidate older query revision identities.

A 256-state asset-history fixture undoes halfway, then branches: cleanup removes
exactly 128 Redo-only retained byte entries and preserves all 128 identities
still reachable through older history, including their exact byte sequences.

A process fixture now retains all three private draft fields, rejects one
oversized draft replacement with the shared resource-limit diagnostic,
retains bytes for two semantic assets, commits text and asset-reference history,
computes default and family-specific command-target material, bounded
identity inspection, constituent success/rejection and combined precondition
review, direct-edit
simulation and preview, version-bound direct-edit proposal, ordered and
caller-bounded batch review with graph-resource and dependency-selection
analysis, grapheme cursor and selection inspection, measured pagination,
fixed-region overflow diagnostics,
and layout-only Export preflight. It also
owns one terminal media job whose registered waveform has a recorded cleanup
failure and remains retry-required. Both
orderly and forced
termination leave a fresh process with empty draft fields, no accepted revision,
history, bytes, or prior media-job authority; derived results and cleanup
bookkeeping exist only in the terminated process. On Linux, both populated
variants add zero writable regular-file descriptors beyond descriptors
inherited from the test harness while all of that state is live.

Browser edits send complete authenticated replacements and use no browser
persistence API.

This evidence establishes raw-byte ownership after a separate ingestion
boundary and several semantic review lifecycles; it does not implement
media decoding, format validation, clipboard or file intake, or temporary
conversion files. The task remains open until the remaining preview and
diagnostic producers, renders, plans, and media operations join the same
lifecycle invariant.

The browser's one-time launch-fragment cleanup and subsequent local navigation
fragment cleanup now use `history.replaceState(null, ...)`. They deliberately
discard any pre-existing History API state rather than serializing it into the
replacement entry while removing the URL fragment. A red-first browser-module
fixture showed the previous implementation propagated a private sentinel in
`history.state`; it also verifies cleanup does not read a throwing state getter.
These are isolated browser policy checks, not proof of all engine persistence.

A red-first browser handoff regression also showed that percent-encoded
`#session%3D` and `#%73ession=` aliases, while correctly rejected for
credential admission, remained in the visible URL with their secret-looking
payload intact. The startup scrub now recognizes the ASCII `session=` prefix
through bounded literal or percent-encoded bytes, including uppercase and
malformed trailing escapes, and clears the fragment without authenticating.
Unrelated navigation fragments remain unchanged at startup; this is URL hygiene,
not a claim that malformed aliases can authorize a session.

A real Firefox navigation fixture now loads encoded, percent-encoded prefix,
and uppercase credential aliases through distinct browsing contexts. It
confirms the actual address bar has no fragment, every private draft control
remains blank and disabled, and no handshake or draft API request occurs.
Reloading the scrubbed tab still cannot obtain session authority.

A follow-up red-first browser-module fixture found that a same-document
`hashchange` after startup could place a fresh `#session=` credential-looking
value in the address bar because only initial navigation was scrubbed. The
runtime now reuses the same bounded literal/encoded-prefix cleanup for later
hash navigation, without accepting or replacing the original in-memory
credential. An independent real Firefox fixture injects canonical and encoded
session-looking fragments into an already authenticated tab, confirms each
URL fragment is erased without additional handshake or draft requests, and
preserves normal `#source-panel` anchor navigation.

The Firefox fixture then reloads the tab without its spent credential,
injects additional canonical and encoded session-looking hashes, and
confirms each new fragment disappears while all draft inputs remain blank
and disabled and no new handshake or draft request occurs. Post-startup
fragments cannot reauthenticate an uncredentialed page.

The authenticated draft replacement transport now requires exactly one
`Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8` field before it decodes and replaces
session text. A red-first adapter regression proved previously admitted POSTs
without a media type or with unrelated types could replace draft fields;
missing, wrong-charset, and duplicate fields now refuse without mutation.

Header-name and media-type case variations remain admitted, and existing
origin, credential, framing, and byte-limit guards remain independent.
This is a wire-contract boundary, not proof of trusted notebook acceptance.

A deterministic byte-mutation regression now varies every byte of the
authenticated draft `Content-Type` field name and declared media type. Only
case-equivalent spelling is admitted; every other mutation leaves all
session-owned draft fields intact, including an existing task value.

An independent loopback socket regression exercises request framing,
response emission, and draft state in the same connection. Authenticated
missing or unrelated media types receive 400 and preserve the full private
draft, while the expected UTF-8 text media type receives 204 and replaces only
the task field.

The browser draft retry control now hides as soon as its final retryable
field succeeds, even when a different field remains blocked by a non-retryable
error. A red-first runtime-extracted browser fixture reproduced the stale
button with zero retryable fields, then verified that another outstanding
retryable field keeps the control available. The terminal failure and its
status remain visible; no failed draft is silently declared synchronized.

The browser now requires `application/json; charset=utf-8` on both HTTP 200
and 409 handshake bodies before attempting JSON parsing. A red-first browser
adapter regression showed that a `200 text/html` carrying otherwise compatible
JSON previously authorized draft hydration. The corrected path leaves editing
disabled and the mismatched body unread. Focused parser coverage pins exact
media admission, mixed-case variants, missing declarations, and wrong charsets.

A real Firefox fixture confirms a `200 text/html` handshake carrying valid
compatible JSON leaves all draft fields blank and disabled, emits no draft
reads, and consumes the launch fragment. A distinct new tab with a correctly
declared JSON handshake then hydrates normally.

This is client-side fail-closed transport evidence; it does not extend the
backend's available capabilities or prove later notebook acceptance.

Browser draft reads now decode the declared UTF-8 body with a fatal decoder
that preserves the leading U+FEFF code point. The previous `Response.text()`
path silently removed that valid character and substituted U+FFFD for malformed
byte sequences. A red-first browser-module fixture and a real Firefox loopback
case verify byte-exact recovery after a valid retry and atomic editing refusal
for invalid source bytes. This is transport fidelity for pre-acceptance drafts,
not a claim that all notebook source and media inputs have been implemented.
